entry.summary:
I've been using PostgreSQL for 9 years and I keep discovering features that would have saved me weeks of application code. Here are the ones I wish I'd learned earlier — each with a real use case from production systems. You have a users table with first_name and last_name. Every query that needs the full name does first_name || ' ' || last_name. Every. Time. ALTER TABLE users ADD COLUMN full_name text GENERATED ALWAYS AS (first_name || ' ' || last_name) STORED;
-- Now you can query, index, and search on full_name directly CREATE INDEX idx_users_full_name ON users USING gin(to_tsvector('english', full_name));
Real use case: We had a products table where price_with_tax was calculated in every API response. Moving it to a generated column eliminated thousands of lines of application-lev…

I've spent the last 4 years reviewing API designs at work, in open-source PRs, and for consulting clients. Some patterns keep appearing — not because developers are bad, but because nobody teaches API design as a discipline.
Here are the 9 mistakes I see most often, with the exact fix for each one.
// GET /users/123 — success
{ "id": 123, "name": "Jane", "email": "jane@example.com" }

// GET /users/999 — not found  
{ "error": "User not found" }

// POST /users — success
{ "message": "User created", "userId": 456 }

// POST /users — validation error
{ "errors": ["Email is required", "Name too short"] }

Four endpoints, four different response shapes. Every consumer has to write custom parsing logic for each one.
The fix: One envelope, every response.
